/-!
# Strict normal crossings boundaries and local parameter equations

Source: Stacks Project, Tags 0BI9 and 0BIA, and ILO, Expose VIII,
Remark 4.2.2(i), printed pp. 118--119
(blueprint: lem-per-snc-boundary-parameter-criterion).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- On a regular Noetherian scheme, a closed subscheme is a strict normal crossings
boundary exactly when its ideal in every local ring is generated by a product of an initial
segment of a regular system of parameters. The ideal is represented on an affine neighbourhood
and extended along the germ map. The empty initial segment has product `1`, covering points
off the boundary and the empty boundary. The boundary predicate includes reducedness,
the effective Cartier condition, and finitely many integral regular components with regular
scheme-theoretic intersections of the expected codimensions.

Source: Stacks Project, Tags 0BI9 and 0BIA; ILO VIII, Remark 4.2.2(i), pp. 118--119
(blueprint: lem-per-snc-boundary-parameter-criterion).

Proof: (1) For a strict normal crossings boundary, each component through a point has a
regular codimension-one local quotient. By
`lem-weil-pbc-regular-local-quotient-kernel-parameters` its ideal is generated by part of a
parameter system; the dimension formula in
`lem-weil-pbc-regular-local-generators-regular-sequence` makes this part have length one.
(2) The intersection dimension conditions and
`lem-weil-pbc-regular-local-cotangent-dimension` make the component equations independent
in the cotangent space. Extend to a basis and apply
`lem-weil-pbc-nakayama-local-generators` to lift it to a regular parameter system.
The UFD and parameter primality statement
`lem-trf-regular-local-ufd-boundary-factorization`, together with radicality, identifies the
boundary ideal with the square-free product of precisely these component parameters.
(3) Conversely, the parameter regular-sequence statement and `lem-sc-regular-local-domain`
make the product a nonzerodivisor. The UFD statement makes its ideal radical, with minimal
primes generated by the selected parameters. Quotients by subsets are regular of dimension
reduced by the subset cardinality. These stalkwise assertions give regular components and
all the required intersections; `lem-weil-pbc-noetherian-finite-components` gives finiteness.
(4) At points outside the boundary use the unit ideal and the empty product. This also gives
the empty boundary, and the converse establishes all the stated Cartier and intersection
consequences. -/
theorem isStrictNormalCrossingsBoundary_iff_parameters (X : Scheme.{u}) [IsNoetherian X]
    (_hX : IsRegularScheme X) (H : X.IdealSheafData) :
    IsStrictNormalCrossingsBoundary H ↔
      ∀ x : X, ∃ (U : X.affineOpens) (hx : x ∈ U.1)
        (t : List (X.presheaf.stalk x)) (r : ℕ),
        IsLocalRing.IsRegularSystemOfParameters t ∧ r ≤ t.length ∧
          (H.ideal U).map (X.presheaf.germ U.1 x hx).hom =
            Ideal.span {(t.take r).prod} := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
